To get the bounding box of a deeply nested SVG object using JavaScript, you can use the `getBBox()` method on the target element. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **Select the Target Element**: Use `document.querySelector` or `document.getElementById` to select the specific SVG element whose bounding box you want to retrieve.

   ```javascript
   const targetElement = document.querySelector('#your-svg-id');
   ```

2. **Get the Bounding Box**: Call the `getBBox()` method on the selected element. This method returns an object with properties like `x`, `y`, `width`, and `height`.

   ```javascript
   const bbox = targetElement.getBBox();
   console.log(bbox);
   ```

3. **Access the Properties**: You can now access the properties of the bounding box object to get the coordinates and dimensions.

   ```javascript
   const x = bbox.x;
   const y = bbox.y;
   const width = bbox.width;
   const height = bbox.height;
   console.log(`X: ${x}, Y: ${y}, Width: ${width}, Height: ${height}`);
   ```

### Example

```html
<svg id="mySvg" width="200" height="200">
  <g>
    <rect id="nestedRect" x="10" y="10" width="50" height="50" fill="blue"/>
  </g>
</svg>

<script>
  const rectElement = document.querySelector('#nestedRect');
  const bbox = rectElement.getBBox();
  console.log(`Bounding Box: X=${bbox.x}, Y=${bbox.y}, Width=${bbox.width}, Height=${bbox.height}`);
</script>
```

This will output the bounding box of the `rect` element, even if it is nested within other elements in the SVG.

### Notes:
- Ensure the SVG element is fully rendered before calling `getBBox()`. If you need to wait for the DOM to load, place your script at the end of the body or use `window.onload`.
- `getBBox()` only works on SVG elements that have been rendered (i.e., not hidden or removed from the DOM).

